  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Consider a deck of $n+1$ distinct cards labelled $1,2,\\ldots,n+1$, with one number on each card.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p28-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How many pairs of cards can be chosen?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\binom{n+1}{2}=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ pairs.",
          "worked": "Choose two cards from the $n+1$ distinct cards; their order does not matter.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{n+1}{2}&=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2!}\\\\\\binom{n+1}{2}&=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p28-q13-b-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "How many pairs of cards can be chosen so that the number ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "k",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " is the ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "lowest",
                  "marks": [
                    "italic"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " number of the two cards?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n+1-k$ pairs.",
          "worked": "The card labelled $k$ is fixed. The other card can be any of $k+1,k+2,\\ldots,n+1$.\n$$\\text{Number of pairs}=(n+1)-k=n+1-k.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q13-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show, giving reasons, why\n$$1+2+\\cdots+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p28-q13-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "1+2+\\cdots+n=\\binom{n+1}{2}=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p28-q13-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Partition all pairs by their lowest card: lowest labels ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "n,n-1,\\ldots,1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " give ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "1,2,\\ldots,n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " pairs respectively.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "By part (a), there are $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ pairs in total.\n\nCount the same pairs according to their lowest card. By part (b), lowest labels $n,n-1,\\ldots,1$ give $1,2,\\ldots,n$ pairs respectively. Every pair has exactly one lowest card, so these cases are disjoint and include every pair.\n\nBoth methods count the same pairs. Hence\n$$1+2+\\cdots+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
  },
